WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.360
- Thanks to Mack Weldon for
keeping Legal Eagle in the air

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.440
and helping me look fly.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.880
You hear it time and time again,

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.820
when Americans are told they
should wear a mask indoors.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.620
- [Man] I'll just put
you on my 3000 follower

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.170
Instagram feed, mostly locals.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:14.003
- Hi, everyone!

00:00:14.003 --> 00:00:15.890
I work for Costco and
I'm asking this member

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:18.400
to put on a mask because
that is our company policy,

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:19.770
so either wear the mask, or--

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.510
- And I'm not doing it 'cause
I woke up in a free country.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:25.690
- A Dallas woman decided to
throw a toddler style tantrum

00:00:25.690 --> 00:00:28.730
in a grocery store.
- I don't give a (bleep)!

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.210
- Not to be outdone by
Dallas, a Fort Worth woman

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:32.700
berated a store clerk.

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.440
- I'm spreading more germs standing here,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:35.830
for God sakes!

00:00:35.830 --> 00:00:37.710
- Suddenly people have doctor's notes

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.370
that prevent them from wearing masks,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:41.940
but the same medical conditions
obviously don't stop them

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:43.350
from screaming.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.040
- My doctor would not let me wear a mask!

00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:47.000
- And this California guy
called the store manager

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.370
the N word and had a meltdown

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.200
over the manager wearing a mask.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.997
- [Man] This guy called me a (bleep).

00:00:52.997 --> 00:00:54.180
- So what?

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.770
- A totally shameless woman wants to call

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.040
the manager of a hospital
because the hospital requires

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:00.600
that all people wear masks.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:01.920
- [Woman] I'm at Saint Joseph's Hospital.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.300
They're telling me that
if I don't wear a mask,

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.340
they're not gonna assist
me, they want me to leave.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.330
- And the people of Florida

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.460
weren't going to be out
Florida-ed by other states,

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.680
so people in Florida challenged mask laws

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.960
because only God can regulate breathing.

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:16.330
- And they went to throw God's

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:19.110
wonderful breathing system out the door.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.060
- I was born free, I will stay free.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.830
My rights come from God, not from you.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.790
- And some people have
more practical reasons

00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.230
for not wearing masks.

00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:28.340
- I don't wear a mask for the same reason

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.910
I don't wear underwear.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:31.400
Things gotta breathe.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.380
- Then there's the Florida man

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.310
who just doesn't feel like
wearing one, for reasons.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:36.770
- I just don't wanna be doing it.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.640
I dunno, somehow sitting
in the oval office

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.120
behind that beautiful resolute desk,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.590
the great resolute desk.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.330
- Half of all states now
require people to wear masks

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:47.380
in public spaces.

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.270
Obviously, a lot of people are
more than a little confused

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.400
about what rights the
constitution of the United States

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.140
grants to its citizens and
what powers the government has

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.220
over its citizens.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.750
Do you have an unalienable right

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:59.630
not to wear a face mask in public?

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:02.260
Can businesses tell
customers to wear masks

00:02:02.260 --> 00:02:04.070
and do those little cards talking about

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:05.700
the Americans with disabilities act

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:07.416
actually have any effect?

00:02:07.416 --> 00:02:09.583
(fanfare)

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:12.243
Hey, Legal Eagles.

00:02:12.243 --> 00:02:13.970
It's time to think like a toddler.

00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:16.520
Nope, nope, it's time
to think like a lawyer.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.370
- In the beginning, God
formed man out of the earth

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:25.370
and breathed his breath in him
and he became a living soul.

00:02:27.130 --> 00:02:29.450
Where do you derive the authority

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.300
to regulate human breathing?

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:34.870
- Well, Florida woman, not
everything that you don't like

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.690
violates your constitutional rights.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.520
For example, we have
freedom of expression,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.570
but there are some things
that we simply cannot do,

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:43.010
like create obscene
materials involving children.

00:02:43.010 --> 00:02:44.210
We have freedom of assembly,

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:45.680
but the government may regulate

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.120
the time, place and manner of
how we exercise that right,

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.480
when they have a legitimate reason.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.950
In short, our constitution
recognizes freedoms with limits.

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:55.010
Under the 10th amendment,
powers not delegated

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:57.090
to the federal government
are reserved to the states

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.290
or to the people.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.900
This means that the
federal government's power

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.700
is not unlimited, so states
and smaller units of government

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:04.790
have the power to regulate certain conduct

00:03:04.790 --> 00:03:06.480
and as we've seen
throughout this pandemic,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.080
the federal government has taken,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.570
let's say, a hands off
approach to dealing with COVID.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.420
- With a federal government,

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.360
we're not supposed to stand on
street corners doing testing.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.890
They go to doctors, they go to hospitals,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.550
they go to the state.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:24.440
The state is a more localized government.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:25.930
You have 50 of them.

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:27.720
- And as a result, most mask orders

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.840
are coming from mayors and governors.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:32.650
Police powers are not
unlimited, but in a pandemic,

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:34.340
governments have much broader authority

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:36.610
to impose regulations that
protect the population

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:38.570
from a virus that has serious implications

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.170
for public health, but at the same time,

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:42.570
citizens have rights
that cannot be taken away

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.130
by any particular laws

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:45.960
and if a law appears to interfere

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:47.210
with a constitutional right,

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:49.520
those whose rights are
affected can challenge that law

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.353
in court.

00:03:50.353 --> 00:03:52.170
Constitutional rights include
the freedom of speech,

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.030
the freedom of religion,

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.500
due process of law and equal
protection under the law.

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:58.060
So because mask laws are so contentious,

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:00.090
let's start with making an analogy.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.050
Let's talk about government
bans on public smoking.

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:04.940
Now, if there isn't a specific right,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.600
then it's highly likely that
the government can outlaw

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:08.860
a certain conduct.

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.870
Take, for instance, bans on smoking.

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:13.070
Smoking is basically like wearing a mask,

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.050
except it's the complete opposite.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:16.730
Obviously, the constitution says nothing

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.160
about a right to smoke.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.110
For smoking to be a constitutional right,

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:21.940
then it would have to fall
under one of the other

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:23.680
recognized constitutional rights.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.300
When governments started banning
smoking in public places,

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.260
smokers actually challenged
those laws on two theories.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.530
One, that smoking is a personal liberty,

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:34.430
protected by the due process
clause of the constitution,

00:04:34.430 --> 00:04:36.520
or two, that the equal protection clause

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.860
extends special protection
to smokers as a group.

00:04:39.860 --> 00:04:41.940
Now, the due process
clause of the constitution

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:44.130
prohibits the government
from depriving individuals

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:47.440
of liberty, without what's
called due process of law.

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:49.010
This means that a legislative body

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.540
must have an adequate
justification for passing a law

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.170
that affects someone's liberty.

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:56.590
Smokers challenged the smoke-free
workplace laws in court

00:04:56.590 --> 00:04:59.150
on the basis that the laws
violated the due process clause.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:01.500
They alleged that the laws
took away a smoker's liberty

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.500
without adequate notice or reason,

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.870
but the courts uniformly
rejected this claim,

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:07.860
since the smoke-free workplace acts

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.940
didn't involve a
fundamental liberty right,

00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:11.530
such as the right to privacy.

00:05:11.530 --> 00:05:14.520
The courts used what's known
as the rational basis test

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.920
to determine whether the
government could regulate

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.410
that particular conduct.

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:20.110
You can think of it like a sliding scale.

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:23.170
The more a law interferes
with a fundamental right,

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.410
the more scrutiny a court will have

00:05:25.410 --> 00:05:27.230
in interpreting that particular law

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.710
and the more it just
involves regular conduct,

00:05:29.710 --> 00:05:31.490
it doesn't involve a fundamental right,

00:05:31.490 --> 00:05:32.710
well, the less scrutiny

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:35.550
and that's generally what
the rational basis test is.

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:38.750
Often cases are won and lost
based on the particular test

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:41.590
that is applied to that law and generally,

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:44.080
if the court applies
a rational basis test,

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.300
that law is almost certainly
going to be upheld.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:48.160
The rational basis test requires simply

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.340
that a law must be rationally related

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:52.570
to a legitimate government goal.

00:05:52.570 --> 00:05:55.433
This requirement sets a very
low bar for the government.

00:05:55.433 --> 00:05:58.330
A law will be considered
constitutional, so long as the law

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:01.240
is not completely irrational or arbitrary.

00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:04.120
Basically, laws never
fail this requirement.

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:06.010
For smokers, this was very bad news.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:07.800
The government had ample
scientific evidence

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.450
that smoking causes health problems

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.410
and secondhand smoke harms other people

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:12.510
who are exposed to it

00:06:12.510 --> 00:06:14.650
and smoking wasn't even
a contagious virus,

00:06:14.650 --> 00:06:16.870
spreading without warning,
so at the end of the day,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.410
it was easy for courts
to uphold smoking bans

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:21.360
because there was a
rational basis to connect

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.220
the smoking bans to the
legitimate public interest

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:25.920
of helping public health.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:26.760
So with that background,

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:29.540
let's talk about COVID-19 mask orders.

00:06:29.540 --> 00:06:33.060
With COVID-19, the CDC estimates
that up to 35% of people

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:36.190
infected have no symptoms,
but are still contagious

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.900
and since masks reduce the
chance of infected people

00:06:38.900 --> 00:06:41.790
transmitting the respiratory
droplets that contain the virus

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:42.840
in enclosed spaces,

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.740
mask orders are on
pretty firm legal ground

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:48.310
and let's look at California
governor, Gavin Newsom's

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:50.040
mask order, as an example.

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:51.820
Governor Newsom ordered
people in California

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.430
to wear face coverings
when they're in, quote,

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:55.530
high risk situations, including inside of,

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.980
or in line to enter any
indoor public space,

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:00.760
visiting a hospital or other
health related building

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.980
and waiting for, or riding
on public transportation

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.930
or ride sharing vehicle.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:08.780
Now, the California emergency
services act is the source

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.310
of the governor's power
to make masks mandatory.

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.960
The law gives the governor
broad authority to respond

00:07:13.960 --> 00:07:17.000
to state emergencies,
like the COVID-19 panic.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:19.190
The governor's authority to
take public health measures

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:21.030
was already approved,
though, by the Supreme Court,

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.060
at least temporarily.

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:24.840
When churches challenged governor
Newsom's temporary orders

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:26.440
on large public gatherings,

00:07:26.440 --> 00:07:28.880
churches sued for an
injunction barring enforcement

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:29.713
of the order.

00:07:29.713 --> 00:07:31.280
The Supreme Court denied their motion

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:32.830
for an emergency injunction.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.680
Chief Justice Roberts
cautioned that courts

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.530
shouldn't second guess emergency measures

00:07:36.530 --> 00:07:38.900
that local governments
imposed for health and safety,

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:40.980
especially, quote, while local officials

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.610
are actively shaping their
response to changing facts

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:44.640
on the ground.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:46.610
Newsom's mask order relies on

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:48.550
the same law and rationale.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:50.233
So what if people sue?

00:07:51.286 --> 00:07:55.250
- [Woman] Target, I'm not
playing any more (bleep) games.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:58.274
This (bleep) is over.

00:07:58.274 --> 00:08:00.315
This (bleep) is all (bleep) over.

00:08:00.315 --> 00:08:03.023
No, uh-uh, no, why, why?

00:08:03.023 --> 00:08:04.324
You let everybody else do it!

00:08:04.324 --> 00:08:05.157
You let everybody else
do it, why can't I do it?

00:08:05.157 --> 00:08:06.667
'Cause I'm a blonde white woman?

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:10.300
- [Man] I think we have enough here.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:11.133
- Okay.

00:08:11.998 --> 00:08:14.070
Listen, ma'am, just spin around,
just put your phone down.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:14.963
- What?
- Okay.

00:08:15.950 --> 00:08:17.410
- Oh my God.
- I'm sorry.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:19.130
- Well, people like
the woman in that video

00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:21.560
will certainly sue Newsom
and other local authorities

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:23.300
who issue a mandatory mask order.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.590
Representatives like
Kentucky's Thomas Massie

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:27.440
thinks that there's no
way that any patriot

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.940
would ever wear a mask or get vaccinated,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.360
which would actually be kind of funny,

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:33.120
if it weren't so sad,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:34.730
because George Washington

00:08:34.730 --> 00:08:36.970
conducted the first
government funded inoculation

00:08:36.970 --> 00:08:40.080
in American history, when he
had the entire continental army

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.720
vaccinated against smallpox.

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.570
Although Representative
Massie and others think that

00:08:43.570 --> 00:08:45.870
face mask orders are unprecedented lunacy

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:47.800
that Americans have
never ever put up with,

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:48.900
how do I put this nicely?

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.410
That is an a-historical interpretation.

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:52.810
Face mask orders were common

00:08:52.810 --> 00:08:55.660
during the 1918, 1919 flu pandemic.

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:57.950
They were enforced with
citations and fines,

00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:00.040
much like authorities are proposing today.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:01.800
Some people were even jailed.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.240
During the influenza pandemic,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.700
there were even influenza courts,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.390
where people could
contest their citations.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.450
Unfortunately, there aren't
really any written decisions

00:09:10.450 --> 00:09:11.330
from that era.

00:09:11.330 --> 00:09:13.230
We do know that there
were pockets of resistance

00:09:13.230 --> 00:09:14.100
to mask wearing,

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.690
but the flu pandemic was
deadlier than world war I,

00:09:16.690 --> 00:09:18.480
which was still raging at the time

00:09:18.480 --> 00:09:20.390
and most people saw wearing a mask

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:21.980
as part of their civic duty.

00:09:21.980 --> 00:09:23.880
When people did challenge the citations,

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.050
judges simply deferred to the government

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:27.720
regarding masks and closures

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:29.750
and that's basically what
Chief Justice Roberts did.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.170
Even though the group
of churches could cite

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:35.430
to a specific fundamental
freedom, in this case,

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:36.320
religious freedom,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:39.010
that theoretically could
have been curtailed

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.960
or at least implicated in these laws,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:43.120
but the fact that we're in
the middle of a pandemic

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.720
really matters and there
is one Supreme Court case

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:47.550
that should probably control the outcome

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:49.350
of all of these different mask lawsuits.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.050
In Jacobson versus Massachusetts,

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:53.120
the court considered whether
the city of Cambridge

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:55.800
could make a smallpox
vaccination mandatory.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.370
The person who sued the city
alleged that the immunization

00:09:58.370 --> 00:10:00.820
violated the equal protection
and due process clauses

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:02.630
and violated the spirit of liberty

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:04.370
embodied by the constitution,

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:05.820
but the Supreme Court held that the city

00:10:05.820 --> 00:10:07.590
was well within its police power authority

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.280
to mandate this immunization.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:12.100
Justice Harlan rejected the
claim that each person is,

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:14.000
quote, a law unto himself

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.680
and could act with no
consideration toward other people.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:18.737
Quote, "The Liberty
secured by the constitution

00:10:18.737 --> 00:10:20.117
"of the United States to every person

00:10:20.117 --> 00:10:21.827
"within his jurisdiction does not import

00:10:21.827 --> 00:10:24.887
"an absolute right in each
person to be, at all times

00:10:24.887 --> 00:10:28.370
"and in all circumstances,
wholly freed from restraint."

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:30.570
Since smallpox cases were
prevalent in Cambridge

00:10:30.570 --> 00:10:31.880
and the disease was increasing,

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.470
the government was justified
in taking responsible measures

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:36.450
to protect citizens from smallpox.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:39.510
Jacobson teaches us that for
a lawsuit against mask orders

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:41.520
to be successful, the
litigants will need to show

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.130
that there is no rational
basis for the law,

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.770
which is an incredibly uphill climb,

00:10:46.770 --> 00:10:49.330
because there's lots of
evidence and arguments

00:10:49.330 --> 00:10:51.990
that mask orders have at
least a rational basis

00:10:51.990 --> 00:10:54.450
to the legitimate aim of public safety.

00:10:54.450 --> 00:10:56.300
There are several strands
of evidence supporting

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:57.350
the efficacy of masks.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.920
Laboratory studies of respiratory droplets

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.990
concluded that masks can block
nearly all of the droplets.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:04.940
Epidemiological studies, like a recent one

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:06.070
published in Health Affairs,

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:09.590
have concluded that masks
slow the COVID-19 growth rate.

00:11:09.590 --> 00:11:12.380
The study compared the COVID-19
growth rate before and after

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:15.170
mask mandates in 15 States
and the district of Columbia.

00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:17.750
It found that the mask
mandates led to a slowdown

00:11:17.750 --> 00:11:20.010
in daily COVID-19 growth,

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:22.490
which became more apparent over time

00:11:22.490 --> 00:11:25.310
and there's lots of other data
out there that I could cite,

00:11:25.310 --> 00:11:28.660
but a quick Google search
will probably turn them up.

00:11:28.660 --> 00:11:30.260
But so far we've just been talking about

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:32.200
the states versus the federal government.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.610
COVID-19 has brought out a
very interesting, if terrible

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:39.830
legal issue as between
governors and municipalities.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:42.040
What happens if governors
and mayors actually

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.730
disagree with each other
about COVID-19 orders,

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.640
or specifically, what
happens when the mayors

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:49.860
want mask orders and the governors say

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:51.900
that there shouldn't be mask orders?

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.450
The outcome of these conflicts
depends on how much power

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:56.850
the cities have under
the Home Rule Doctrine.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.750
Home rule is the idea that
municipalities and counties

00:11:59.750 --> 00:12:03.000
have autonomy to act with
respect to their own affairs.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.730
40 of the 50 States operate
under Dillon's Rule,

00:12:05.730 --> 00:12:07.550
which means that municipalities only have

00:12:07.550 --> 00:12:09.300
specifically enumerated powers.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.850
In other words, the municipalities,

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.880
like cities and counties,
only have the power

00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:16.520
that the state governments
actually bestow upon them.

00:12:16.520 --> 00:12:19.210
This is why Georgia governor,
Brian Kemp, filed a lawsuit

00:12:19.210 --> 00:12:21.420
to stop Atlanta and 14
other Georgia cities

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.300
from making masks mandatory.

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.580
Kemp asked a judge to
overturn Atlanta mayor,

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:27.270
Keisha Lance Bottom's orders,

00:12:27.270 --> 00:12:29.400
that were more restrictive than his own,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:31.230
block her from issuing additional orders

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:34.210
and instruct the city council
not to ratify her actions

00:12:34.210 --> 00:12:36.220
and force her not to make
any public statements

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:38.850
claiming she has authority
that exceeds Kemp's.

00:12:38.850 --> 00:12:40.920
Now so far, the Georgia mayors have vowed

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:42.983
to defy Kemp's orders
and fight his lawsuit,

00:12:42.983 --> 00:12:45.740
so that they can actually
make masks mandatory,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.450
to stop the spread of
COVID-19, but in Georgia,

00:12:48.450 --> 00:12:50.450
the home rule says that the city,

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:52.750
whether it's the mayor
or the city council,

00:12:52.750 --> 00:12:55.560
can only do the things
that the state government,

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.710
through the governor, allows them to do,

00:12:57.710 --> 00:13:00.710
so it's likely that they
may lose those lawsuits.

00:13:00.710 --> 00:13:02.690
In contrast, a few
states give municipality

00:13:02.690 --> 00:13:05.030
significantly greater police powers.

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:07.760
In Alaska, which observes
the minority rule,

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:09.280
a home rule city, quote,

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:11.160
may exercise all legislative powers,

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.730
not prohibited by law or by charter.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:17.680
So instead of a city needing
the power granted to them

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.600
by the state government,

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:20.960
the cities can do whatever they want,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.900
unless it's prohibited
by the state government

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.960
and Anchorage issued a
mandatory mask rule in June

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:28.990
and although the governor's
office tried to limit

00:13:28.990 --> 00:13:31.550
the city's authority to mandate
masks in state buildings,

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:33.870
Alaska's legislature has not restricted

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.130
Anchorage's authority
regarding mandatory masks

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.420
or exempt state owned buildings.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:41.180
This means that Anchorage's
order will likely stand.

00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:43.200
So that's states versus cities,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:45.550
but by now you've probably
seen a lot of people claiming

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:47.130
that mask orders conflict

00:13:47.130 --> 00:13:49.890
with the Americans with
disabilities act or ADA.

00:13:49.890 --> 00:13:52.260
So what about people who
claim they cannot wear masks

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:53.520
for health reasons?

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.980
Well, title two of the
ADA generally prohibits

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:57.600
eligibility and screening criteria

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.940
that would tend to exclude
individuals based on disability,

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:03.390
unless the criteria are
necessary for the business

00:14:03.390 --> 00:14:05.900
to operate safely in providing
its goods and services.

00:14:05.900 --> 00:14:08.330
Those requirements must
be based on actual risks

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:10.640
and may not be based on
speculation, stereotypes

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.000
or generalization about
people with disabilities.

00:14:13.000 --> 00:14:14.850
Businesses are safe, generally,

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:18.090
in relying on government
mask orders and CDC guidance

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:20.800
in refusing entry to people without masks

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.970
and if a person without a mask enters,

00:14:22.970 --> 00:14:26.180
the ADA only allows a retailer
to deny goods or services

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:27.800
to an individual with a disability

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:30.450
if their presence would result in, quote,

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.880
a direct threat to the
health and safety of others,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.140
but only when this threat
cannot be eliminated

00:14:35.140 --> 00:14:38.290
by modifying existing policies,
practices and procedures,

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:40.730
or permitting another
type of accommodation

00:14:40.730 --> 00:14:43.050
and whether a customer
poses a direct threat

00:14:43.050 --> 00:14:45.610
is an individualized
fact sensitive inquiry,

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.150
but you can imagine
that during a pandemic,

00:14:48.150 --> 00:14:51.320
having an unmasked person
spreading, potentially,

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:55.190
droplets that carry the
virus, could certainly qualify

00:14:55.190 --> 00:14:57.010
and that puts businesses in a tough spot,

00:14:57.010 --> 00:14:59.440
because generally, they
need to accommodate people

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:02.250
with disabilities, but they
cannot let that accommodation

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.990
directly threaten all
of the other customers,

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.080
but in many situations,

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:08.430
there might be accommodations
that can be offered that,

00:15:08.430 --> 00:15:11.180
while it wouldn't allow a
person to enter a building

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:12.560
without a mask on,

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:14.430
they might be able to get
the goods and services

00:15:14.430 --> 00:15:15.350
in a different way.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:16.910
For example, in one scenario,

00:15:16.910 --> 00:15:18.880
a manager offered a woman accommodations

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:21.789
that didn't involve wearing a
mask, like shopping for her.

00:15:21.789 --> 00:15:22.622
- [Manager] Okay, can we shop for you?

00:15:22.622 --> 00:15:24.330
- [Woman] So what does that look like?

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:25.640
- Not allowing her into the building,

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.990
but conducting the shopping on her behalf.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:29.830
Now we tend to focus on the kind of people

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:32.500
who are clearly capable of wearing masks,

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:36.250
but choose not to, either
because they are jerks or insane.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:38.680
The kind of people who
claim that a cloth mask

00:15:38.680 --> 00:15:43.680
will trap the gas, CO2 and
also prevent the gas, oxygen,

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:47.003
from getting into your mouth.

00:15:47.920 --> 00:15:49.900
That's not how that works,

00:15:49.900 --> 00:15:51.920
but that's not to say
that there aren't people

00:15:51.920 --> 00:15:54.760
with legitimate ADA
recognized disabilities

00:15:54.760 --> 00:15:57.460
that might be incapable of wearing a mask.

00:15:57.460 --> 00:15:59.910
For example, you might think of, perhaps,

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:02.130
someone with autism that cannot function

00:16:02.130 --> 00:16:03.960
with restrictions over their face,

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.750
which is why the ADA
exists in the first place

00:16:06.750 --> 00:16:09.340
and that's why
accommodations should be made

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:10.440
if it's at all possible.

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:12.090
Things like online ordering,

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:14.230
contactless deliveries, curbside pickup

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:16.730
and even private browsing
at a specific time

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:18.950
are all potentially
reasonable alternatives

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:21.790
for businesses to use,

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:24.530
but unfortunately,
because this is America,

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:26.450
it sure looks as if millions of Americans

00:16:26.450 --> 00:16:28.300
are suddenly inventing medical reasons

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:30.990
for resisting mask orders,
without adequate proof

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:32.950
and as usual, people are
trying to make a buck

00:16:32.950 --> 00:16:34.440
by selling identification cards

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:36.160
stating that the holder has a disability

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.500
and cannot wear a mask.

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:41.540
These face mask exempt cards
are all over social media.

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:44.050
None of these have any
legal force whatsoever,

00:16:44.050 --> 00:16:46.340
even the federal government
is wary of these scammers.

00:16:46.340 --> 00:16:48.500
The department of Justice issued a warning

00:16:48.500 --> 00:16:50.910
that any flyer stating that
people with disabilities

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.770
do not need to comply with
face mask laws are fraudulent,

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:55.140
especially flyers that include

00:16:55.140 --> 00:16:57.610
the Department of Justice's
name and official seal,

00:16:57.610 --> 00:17:00.360
but businesses find themselves
on the horn of a dilemma.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:03.300
On the one hand, they have
to comply with the ADA,

00:17:03.300 --> 00:17:04.133
but on the other hand,

00:17:04.133 --> 00:17:07.490
they also can't be negligent
and lead to the infection

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:08.950
of a whole bunch of other people.

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:11.700
There is a whole lot of
controversy over potentially

00:17:11.700 --> 00:17:14.170
making businesses exempt from liability

00:17:14.170 --> 00:17:17.220
related to COVID if
certain legislation passes,

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:18.640
but at the moment,

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.440
businesses cannot act
in a negligent manner.

00:17:21.440 --> 00:17:24.290
That's how negligence
works in the United States

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.170
and we probably, as customers,

00:17:26.170 --> 00:17:28.160
don't want them to
behave in a negligent way

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:31.210
and if they do, then they
might be liable for damages

00:17:31.210 --> 00:17:33.750
in causing other people to get infected

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:35.480
if they acted in a negligent way,

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:38.780
which is why in the absence
of law to the contrary,

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:41.490
businesses can certainly require employees

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:43.530
and customers to wear masks,

00:17:43.530 --> 00:17:46.700
even apart from what is
mandated by the state

00:17:46.700 --> 00:17:47.770
or federal government.

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:51.860
- Every single one of you that
are obeying the devil's laws

00:17:51.860 --> 00:17:53.600
are going to be arrested.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.240
- Not so fast, Florida woman.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:57.770
Businesses can require
customers and employees

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.260
to wear masks and some
businesses have signs,

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:01.337
that you might've seen, that say,

00:18:01.337 --> 00:18:03.480
"No shirt, no shoes, no service."

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:06.110
That's because businesses
can set their own rules,

00:18:06.110 --> 00:18:08.390
because they're on private property,

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.810
as long as they don't violate
laws or public health rules

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:13.300
and there's certainly
an argument to be made

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:17.270
